MON syntax is implemented for supplied arguments and compile-time anonymous
const records, including inline nested values. Runtime anonymous records remain
a separate deferred feature. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

MON means Moth Object Notation. **MON syntax** names Moth's shared parenthesised
argument and value-construction notation. **The MON format** is the separate
serialised-data profile with its own supported values and serialisation rules.
Using MON syntax does not make a value serialisable or turn a call into a data document.

This reference owns the shared notation and the distinction between grouping,
supplied arguments and anonymous construction. Calls, constructors, directives
and const or runtime records retain their own semantic contracts.

### Declarations and supplied values

Pipes enclose declared parameters and typed nominal fields. Parentheses supply
values or group an expression.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Size = |
        width Int,
        height Int,
    |

    size = Size(width = 1280, height = 720)
]

A nominal value uses its named constructor. Matching field names do not make
an anonymous record a `Size`. MON syntax adds no implicit nominal construction,
structural typing, argument spreading or tuple values.

Declaration lists remain flat. A legal default expression may contain nested
value construction without introducing a nested type declaration. Defaults
still need the type and compile-time evidence their receiving declaration requires.

### Grouping and records

A single unnamed expression in parentheses is grouping. A named initializer
selects anonymous record construction, including a record with one field.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    total #= (1 + 2)
    label #= (name = "Moth")
    labels #= (first = "Priya", second = "Rob",)
    empty #= ()
]

A comma separates entries. It does not create a tuple or infer a field name.
`(value,)`, `(first, second)` and `(,)` are invalid value constructions.
Expression-position `()` is an empty const record when the receiving context
requires a compile-time value. Empty runtime records are outside the initial
runtime surface. Neither case introduces a unit type.

`(calculate(x = 1, y = 2))` groups a call. Names and commas inside that inner
call do not classify the outer parentheses. Grouping preserves ordinary
precedence, place access, postfix operations and immediate cast-target rules.

### Entries and layout

Every anonymous entry has a unique identifier and an explicit
`label = expression` entry. It requires at least one horizontal whitespace
character before `=` and whitespace immediately after it. Its type is inferred
from the expression. Entries have no type annotation, declaration qualifier,
default, omitted value or field-level mutable declaration. There are no
positional anonymous fields or implicit name shorthands.

Use commas between entries. One trailing comma is allowed. Blank lines and
comments may separate entries. The label and `=` must share one logical line.
LF and CR are the only line breaks, with CRLF counting as one break; every
other Unicode whitespace character is horizontal whitespace and may separate
them. At least one whitespace character must immediately follow `=`, and it
may be a line break, so the value can start on the next line. The same
named-entry boundary applies to MON, including implicit and explicit roots,
nested records and qualified payloads. Newlines do not replace commas or join
an incomplete label to a later assignment.

Map entry `=` also requires whitespace on both sides. MON's `{=}` empty-map
spelling is the exception because it is not a non-empty map entry.

A field label does not bind a local name. Every initializer resolves names in
the surrounding scope. Earlier fields are not bare names visible to later fields.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    title #= "Strategy"
    panel #= (
        title = title,
        details = (title = "Overview"),
    )
]

The two `title` labels belong to different records and do not conflict. Duplicate
labels within the same record are invalid.

Each initializer supplies one value. Multiple return slots cannot become a tuple
field or expand into several entries. A field label supplies no expected type
for an otherwise untyped `none`, empty container or `cast`. Use an earlier typed
value when its type cannot be inferred. The existing expression rules decide
which empty literals can infer their own type.

### Recursive construction

Anonymous values nest directly. Named nominal children still use their constructors.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Size = |
        width Int,
        height Int,
    |

    settings #= (
        window = (
            title = "Strategy",
            size = Size(width = 1280, height = 720),
        ),
    )

    width #= settings.window.size.width
]

`#=` requires the complete value to fold, including every nested field. It does
not grant new compile-time execution capabilities. A runtime receiving context
selects the separate runtime-record contract even when all fields look constant.

@../constants/#const-records (Const records) owns folded fields and runtime-use
restrictions. @../structs/#anonymous-records (Runtime anonymous records) owns
hidden nominal identity, local composition and escape restrictions.

### Context determines argument policy

Calls, constructors and directives share MON list syntax without sharing every
permitted argument or value category.

- User calls and named constructors keep positional-then-named signature routing.
- Host calls and builtin members keep their positional-only contracts.
- Anonymous records require named entries and establish their own fields.
- Directive arguments require compile-time values and their registered categories.

A known signature supplies parameter names, types and defaults. An anonymous
record has no callee signature or omitted default slots. Shared syntax changes
neither evaluation order nor the ordinary access, coercion and error rules.

`consume(x = 1)` supplies the argument named `x`. `consume((x = 1))` supplies one
record expression, whose legality still depends on record and parameter rules.
There is no automatic record-to-argument expansion.

Ordinary `f()` is a zero-argument call. The accepted directive contract uses
bare `$name` when no arguments are supplied and rejects `$name()`. Template
labels, indices and helper wrappers retain their specialised meanings.

### Shared construction parity

For a supported common literal form in the same language and format revision,
equivalent receiving types or schemas and the same numeric profile require both
parsers to accept the form and produce the same typed value. Source and MON keep
independent parsers: compiler-local owners parse calls and declarations, while
`moth-mon` parses literal data. Both consume identifier, reserved-word and numeric
text facts from `moth-lexical`. The real-source conformance suite at
`src/compiler_tests/mon_syntax_parity/` checks each expected outcome before
comparing typed values.

Nominal constructor names and choice variant names take a payload only when
`(` follows on the same logical line with only horizontal whitespace between.
LF, CR or a line comment between the head and `(` means there is no payload;
the following input fails through ordinary parsing. Source and MON share this
head rule. Newlines inside argument lists and after `=` remain valid, and the
existing adjacency restriction between `::` and its variant name is unchanged.

Common invalid forms must fail in both parsers for the expected reason.
Intentional data/source differences include:
- MON frames one root record, including implicit and empty roots. Source
  receivers also accept expression fragments.
- A MON schema supplies field and nominal shape. Source infers anonymous
  records and requires explicit nominal constructors.
- MON contains literal values only. Expressions, bindings, imports, mutable
  access and declarations remain source syntax.
- MON preserves raw quoted text and uses its own bounded escapes. Source quoted
  slices have separate escape and line-ending rules.
- MON applies codec text, nesting, node and materialisation budgets. These are
  not source grammar limits.

Accepted but undelivered source capabilities remain named test gaps and
progress/roadmap items. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

Moth-source `{=}` empty-map literals, Unicode escapes and contextual
`::Variant` construction remain undelivered. MON's corresponding data forms do
not deliver those source capabilities.

This contract does not make an arbitrary MON document pasteable anywhere in
Moth. Document framing, supplied schema information and legal source receiving
contexts still matter. An anonymous source record does not implicitly construct
a nominal struct just because a MON schema can receive that record as struct
data.

### MON format boundary

Moth MON source syntax allows ordinary expressions according to the receiving
Moth context. MON serialised data is self-contained literal data only. A MON
reader never evaluates Moth expressions, including foldable ones.

@../mon/#mon-format (MON format) owns the serialised literal-data contract:
one root record, literal-only values, strict numeric conversion, qualifiers,
defaults and bounded first errors. This reference owns the shared notation and
the distinction between grouping, supplied arguments and anonymous
construction.

The standalone `moth_mon` codec serves independent Rust callers. Compiler
clients can use the same API and Rust types through the convenience re-export
`moth::mon`. See @../progress (Progress) for current support. Moth-native
`$mon`, automatic schema extraction and the static project builder remain
deferred separately. The engine or application supplies its own data schema.

Full compile-time Moth authoring expressions and the self-contained serialised
representation are distinct surfaces.

Templates remain ordinary strings. MON syntax adds no automatic record printing,
implicit live bindings, stored Wire capabilities or executable Route values.
